  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">HIV &amp; AIDS  Prevention Programme for MSM &amp; SW in Kenya</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Men who have sex with men and sex workers have disproportionately high levels of HIV in Kenya, accounting for almost a third of all new HIV infections. Because their risk behaviour is stigmatised and/or illegal, they are driven underground and are not reached by mainstream HIV programmes.  These populations have been identified in Kenya&apos;s national HIV strategy as requiring more targeted HIV prevention and care services. Sex workers and men who have sex with men will be trained to reach their peers with targeted and appropriate HIV testing, counselling and other services. Condoms, suitable lubricants, and targeted appropriate education materials will help people reduce their risk of HIV. Health workers serving these groups will be trained to provide non-discriminatory care. Partners will challenge policies and laws that criminalise and discriminate against these groups. Activities will focus in Nyanza and Nairobi, two regions of Kenya with the highest HIV prevalence. These activities will ensure that these marginalised groups can protect themselves from HIV, get tested, and if positive, are not stigmatised or discriminated against when they use mainstream care and support services. There will also be opportunities provided for vocational training and access to micro-credit schemes for this vulnerable group.</narrative>
  </description>
